# Copilot Prompt — Fix Resume Layout (Full-Width Wrapping)

## The Problem

Currently, the resume uses a strict Flexbox two-column layout (`display: flex` on `.resume-body`). 
This creates a major issue: when the left column (Experience, Projects, Volunteering) is much longer than the right column (Education, Skills, Links), it leaves a huge empty blank space under the right column. 

## The Goal

We want the left column to automatically expand to take up **full 100% width** as soon as it clears the bottom of the right column. This is a classic "masonry" or "magazine wrap" layout that maximizes space on a resume.

## The Solution: The "Float Wrap" Technique

We can achieve this beautifully without Javascript by using the classic CSS `float` property. If we float the right column and let the left column behave as a normal block, the left column's text will naturally flow around the right column!

To make this work seamlessly with our blue section borders (`border-bottom`), we will give the right column a solid white background and use `padding-left` instead of margin, so it neatly covers the lines from the left side without them bleeding through.

---

### Step 1: Update the CSS in `resume_pdf_template.html`

Find the layout CSS (around line 75-90) and replace it completely with this:

```css
    /* 1. Change from Flexbox to Block with Clearfix */
    .resume-body {
      display: block;
    }
    .resume-body::after {
      content: "";
      display: table;
      clear: both;
    }

    /* 2. Float the right column and add white background */
    .col-right {
      float: right;
      width: 36%;
      max-width: 36%;
      /* Use padding instead of margin so the white background covers the gap */
      padding-left: 24px;
      padding-bottom: 24px;
      background-color: #fff;
      position: relative;
      z-index: 10;
      box-sizing: border-box;
    }

    /* 3. Let left column flow naturally (no width constraints) */
    .col-left {
      display: block;
      /* Remove flex, width, and max-width properties */
    }
```

### Step 2: Swap the Column Order in the HTML

For a `float` to affect the flow of a subsequent element, **the floated element MUST come first in the HTML DOM.**

Find `<div class="resume-body">` in `resume_pdf_template.html`. You must cut the entire `<div class="col-right">...</div>` block and paste it **above** the `<div class="col-left">...</div>` block.

**Change from this:**
```html
    <div class="resume-body">
      <div class="col-left">
        <!-- Experience, Projects, etc. -->
      </div>
      
      <div class="col-right">
        <!-- Education, Skills, etc. -->
      </div>
    </div>
```

**To this exact structure:**
```html
    <div class="resume-body">
      <!-- 1. RIGHT COLUMN MUST BE FIRST -->
      <div class="col-right">
        {% if resume.education %}
        <div class="section-heading">Education</div>
        ...
        <!-- All other right column content -->
      </div>

      <!-- 2. LEFT COLUMN MUST BE SECOND -->
      <div class="col-left">
        {% if resume.experience %}
        <div class="section-heading">Experience</div>
        ...
        <!-- All other left column content -->
      </div>
    </div>
```

### Why this specific technique works:
1. Because `.col-right` is `float: right`, `.col-left` (which is 100% wide) flows underneath it.
2. The blue `border-bottom` from `.section-heading` inside `.col-left` will technically span 100% of the page width.
3. However, because `.col-right` has `background-color: #fff; position: relative; z-index: 10;`, it sits *on top* of those lines and perfectly hides them on the right side.
4. Because we use `padding-left: 24px` instead of `margin`, the white background covers the gap, meaning the blue line from the left stops exactly 24px away from the right text.
5. As soon as the left content passes the bottom of `.col-right`, the text and the blue section lines will naturally extend to the full 100% width of the page!
